\title{A Guarded Transfer Principle for Real Analysis\\ over a Total Four-Constructor Arithmetic}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{23 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study the extent to which ordinary real analysis survives passage into a \emph{total} arithmetic: the four-constructor \emph{transreal carrier}
\[
  \T \;=\; \{\fin x : x \in \R\} \cup \{+\infty\} \cup \{-\infty\} \cup \{\nul\},
\]
equipped with addition, negation, multiplication, reciprocal and division defined on \emph{all} inputs, the exceptional element $\nul$ (``nullity'') absorbing the indeterminate forms $\infty-\infty$, $0\cdot\infty$ and $0/0$.

Our main theorem is a \emph{guarded transfer principle}. Formalising ``theorem built from continuous real functions by composition, addition, multiplication and division'' as an inductive expression syntax with a real and a total transreal semantics, we prove that every expression whose denominator subexpressions are nowhere vanishing has transreal semantics equal to $\fin \circ (\text{real semantics})$, hence continuous into $\T$ under the natural compact Hausdorff topology. Transfer is moreover \emph{exactly conservative} and \emph{functorial} under continuous reparametrisation.

We then show the guard is sharp and canonical. Four independent characterisations select the same denominators: the units of the total multiplication; the exact continuity locus of the binary division map on the finite square; a rigidity theorem showing that for every $T_1$ topology the punctured constant $x\mapsto x/x$ admits a unique continuous repair value, namely $1$, which the arithmetic refuses; and the non-repairability of the reciprocal by any element of the carrier.

Finally we compute the exact boundary of failure: at an isolated denominator zero there is a trichotomy in which coincident zeros always destroy continuity (with no regularity hypotheses), sign-changing poles destroy it, and one-signed poles with a positive denominator \emph{preserve} it --- so that $x\mapsto 1/x^{2}$ is a continuous map $\R\to\T$ with value $+\infty$ at the origin. The positivity is essential: since the total reciprocal of zero is $+\infty$ by convention, the value assigned at a pole follows the sign of the numerator alone, so a one-signed pole with a negative denominator also fails; strikingly, $1/(-x^{2})$ and $(-1)/x^{2}$ agree as real functions off the origin yet are assigned $+\infty$ and $-\infty$ there, only the second being continuous. Hence the guard is sufficient and syntactically unimprovable but not necessary, and the unguarded failure is generic rather than universal. We close with the structural cost of totality --- failure of joint continuity of $+$ and $\cdot$, and of distributivity --- and with applications to exception-free arithmetic in cryptographic implementations.
\end{abstract}

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} transreal arithmetic, nullity, total division, transfer principle, two-point compactification, continuity locus, pole trichotomy, exception-free computation.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Totality versus faithfulness}

Division is the only partial operation of elementary arithmetic, and its partiality is the origin of an outsized share of engineering pain: hardware traps, exception handlers, data-dependent branches, and---in cryptographic code---timing side channels. A recurring proposal is therefore to \emph{totalise}: extend the number system with enough exceptional values that every operation returns something, so that programs never trap and formulas never need side conditions.

The best-known such extension adjoins to $\R$ two signed infinities and a single further element $\nul$ (\emph{nullity}), and posits
\[
  \frac{1}{0} = +\infty, \qquad \frac{-1}{0} = -\infty, \qquad \frac{0}{0} = \nul .
\]
IEEE-754 floating point makes a closely related choice, with $\nul$ playing the role of \texttt{NaN}.

Totalisation is cheap to state and easy to implement. The mathematical question it raises is \emph{faithfulness}: if a theorem is proved about real numbers and then re-read in the totalised system, does it remain true, and if not, exactly where does it break? A totalisation that quietly changes truth values is worse than useless; one that provably does not is a licence to reason classically and implement branch-free.

\subsection{The conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Informal]
Every theorem built from continuous real functions using finite composition, addition, multiplication, and division by a nowhere-zero denominator transfers through the finite transreal fragment; while any unguarded extension allowing a denominator to reach zero generally fails to preserve continuity into a natural Hausdorff topology on the four-constructor carrier.
\end{conjecture}

We prove this in a strong form. Both hedges turn out to be exactly right: the guard cannot be weakened (Section~\ref{sec:sharp}), and ``generally'' cannot be strengthened to ``always'', because of the one-signed pole regime (Section~\ref{sec:poles}).

\section{The carrier and its total arithmetic}

\begin{definition}[Transreal carrier]
$\T$ is the four-constructor type
\[
  \T \;::=\; \fin(x:\R) \;\mid\; +\infty \;\mid\; -\infty \;\mid\; \nul .
\]
The map $\fin : \R\to\T$ is injective; its image is the \emph{finite fragment}, and its elements are called \emph{finite}.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Addition]
$\nul$ is absorbing: $\nul + a = a + \nul = \nul$. Otherwise
\[
 \fin x + \fin y = \fin(x+y), \quad \fin x \pm \infty = \pm\infty + \fin x = \pm\infty,
\]
\[
 (+\infty)+(+\infty) = +\infty, \quad (-\infty)+(-\infty) = -\infty, \quad (+\infty)+(-\infty) = (-\infty)+(+\infty) = \nul .
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Negation]
$-\fin x = \fin(-x)$, $-(+\infty) = -\infty$, $-(-\infty) = +\infty$, $-\nul = \nul$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Multiplication]
$\nul$ is absorbing; $\fin x \cdot \fin y = \fin(xy)$; and
\[
 \fin x \cdot (+\infty) = \begin{cases}\nul & x=0\\ +\infty & x>0\\ -\infty & x<0\end{cases}
 \qquad
 \fin x \cdot (-\infty) = \begin{cases}\nul & x=0\\ -\infty & x>0\\ +\infty & x<0\end{cases}
\]
symmetrically on the other side, with $(\pm\infty)\cdot(\pm\infty)$ given by the sign rule.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Reciprocal and division]
\[
 \recip(\fin x) = \begin{cases} +\infty & x = 0\\ \fin x^{-1} & x \neq 0,\end{cases}
 \qquad \recip(\pm\infty) = \fin 0, \qquad \recip(\nul) = \nul,
\]
and $a/b := a\cdot\recip(b)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
Addition on $\T$ is commutative and associative with identity $\fin 0$; multiplication is commutative with identity $\fin 1$; and $-(-a) = a$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Exhaustive case analysis on constructors; the finite--finite cases reduce to the corresponding real laws.
\end{proof}

We write $0$ and $1$ for $\fin 0$ and $\fin 1$ where no confusion arises.

\begin{theorem}[Exact conservativity of guarded arithmetic]\label{thm:cons}
For all $x,y\in\R$,
\[
 \fin x + \fin y = \fin(x+y), \qquad \fin x \cdot \fin y = \fin(xy), \qquad -\fin x = \fin(-x),
\]
and, provided $y\neq 0$, $\;\dfrac{\fin x}{\fin y} = \fin\!\left(\dfrac{x}{y}\right)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The first three are defining clauses. For the fourth, $y\neq0$ gives $\recip(\fin y) = \fin y^{-1}$, so $\fin x/\fin y = \fin(xy^{-1}) = \fin(x/y)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The division boundary]\label{thm:boundary}
For every $x\in\R$,
\[
 \frac{\fin x}{\fin 0} = \begin{cases} +\infty & x>0,\\ -\infty & x<0,\\ \nul & x=0.\end{cases}
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\recip(\fin 0) = +\infty$, so the quotient is $\fin x\cdot(+\infty)$; apply the sign clauses of the multiplication table.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Self-division]\label{cor:selfdiv}
$\dfrac{\fin x}{\fin x} = \nul$ if $x = 0$, and $=1$ otherwise.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Closure and its failure]
The finite fragment is closed under $+$, $\cdot$, $-$, and under division by a nonzero denominator; it is \emph{not} closed under division by $0$, since $\fin x/\fin 0 \in \{+\infty,-\infty,\nul\}$ for every $x$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{definition}[Strict lift]
For $f:\R\to\R$, the strict lift $\widehat f : \T\to\T$ sends $\fin x \mapsto \fin(f x)$ and every exceptional element to $\nul$. Strict lifts compose on the nose: $\widehat f\circ\widehat g = \widehat{f\circ g}$.
\end{definition}

\section{The natural topology}

Let $\Rbar = [-\infty,+\infty]$ be the two-point compactification of the line with its order topology. Topologise $\T$ by pulling back the disjoint-union topology along the bijection
\[
 \iota : \T \longrightarrow \Rbar \sqcup \{\ast\}, \qquad \fin x\mapsto x, \quad \pm\infty\mapsto\pm\infty, \quad \nul \mapsto \ast .
\]

\begin{theorem}[The natural topology]\label{thm:top}
With this topology:
\begin{enumerate}
 \item $\T$ is Hausdorff;
 \item $\T$ is compact;
 \item $\fin : \R\to\T$ is an open embedding;
 \item $\{\nul\}$ is open, i.e.\ nullity is isolated;
 \item $\fin t \to +\infty$ as $t\to+\infty$ and $\fin t\to-\infty$ as $t\to-\infty$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\iota$ is a bijection onto a compact Hausdorff space and the induced topology makes it a closed embedding with full range, so Hausdorffness and compactness transfer. Item (3) holds because $\R\hookrightarrow\Rbar$ and $\Rbar\hookrightarrow\Rbar\sqcup\{\ast\}$ are open embeddings; (4) is openness of the second summand; (5) is the corresponding fact in $\Rbar$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Nullity fibres are open]\label{cor:fibre}
If $h : X\to\T$ is continuous then $h^{-1}(\{\nul\})$ is open.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
Three demands pin the topology down morally: the finite fragment must be an open copy of $\R$; $\pm\infty$ must be the two ends of the line; and $\nul$ must be isolated, since nullity is not the limit of finite values under any arithmetic law---it is a flag, not a magnitude. The positive results below use only these properties, and the strongest negative results use only $T_1$ separation.
\end{remark}

\section{The transfer principle}

\begin{definition}[Expressions]
For a parameter space $X$, the set $\mathsf{Expr}(X)$ is generated inductively by atoms $\mathrm{atom}\,f$ ($f:X\to\R$), constants $\mathrm{const}\,c$, compositions $\mathrm{comp}\,f\,e$ ($f:\R\to\R$), and the binary formers $e_1+e_2$, $e_1\cdot e_2$, $e_1/e_2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Two semantics]
The \emph{real semantics} $\Rev{e} : X\to\R$ interprets the constructors by ordinary real arithmetic (with the irrelevant convention $c/0 = 0$). The \emph{transreal semantics} $\Tev{e} : X\to\T$ interprets $\mathrm{atom}\,f$ as $\fin\circ f$, $\mathrm{const}\,c$ as the constant $\fin c$, $\mathrm{comp}\,f$ as the strict lift $\widehat f$, and $+,\cdot,/$ by total transreal arithmetic.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Pointwise guard]
$\Def(e,x)$ holds iff every denominator subexpression of $e$ has nonzero real value at $x$: it is $\top$ on atoms and constants, propagates through $\mathrm{comp}$, $+$ and $\cdot$, and for $e_1/e_2$ requires $\Def(e_1,x)$, $\Def(e_2,x)$ and $\Rev{e_2}(x)\neq0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Uniform guard]\label{def:guard}
For $X$ a topological space, $e$ is \emph{guarded} iff every atom and every composed function is continuous and, for every division subexpression $e_1/e_2$, $\Rev{e_2}(x)\neq0$ for \emph{all} $x\in X$. It is \emph{weakly guarded} iff the same holds with the nowhere-vanishing clause deleted.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Pointwise exact conservativity]\label{thm:pointwise}
If $\Def(e,x)$ then $\Tev{e}(x) = \fin\big(\Rev{e}(x)\big)$. In particular the value is finite: the exceptional constructors are unreachable under the guard.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Structural induction. Atoms and constants are immediate. For $\mathrm{comp}\,f\,e$ the inductive hypothesis places the argument in the finite fragment, where the strict lift acts as $f$. For $+$ and $\cdot$ apply Theorem~\ref{thm:cons}. The only nontrivial case is $e_1/e_2$, where the guard supplies $\Rev{e_2}(x)\neq0$ and the guarded division clause of Theorem~\ref{thm:cons} applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Guarded transfer principle]\label{thm:transfer}
Let $X$ be a topological space and $e$ guarded. Then $\Rev{e}$ is continuous, $\Tev{e} = \fin\circ\Rev{e}$, and hence $\Tev{e} : X\to\T$ is continuous into the compact Hausdorff carrier.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Continuity of $\Rev{e}$ is a structural induction using continuity of sums, products, compositions and of quotients with nowhere-vanishing denominator. The identity is Theorem~\ref{thm:pointwise} at every point; continuity follows since $\fin$ is continuous by Theorem~\ref{thm:top}(3).
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Faithfulness]\label{thm:faithful}
For guarded $e_1,e_2$,
\[
 \big(\forall x,\ \Tev{e_1}(x) = \Tev{e_2}(x)\big) \iff \big(\forall x,\ \Rev{e_1}(x) = \Rev{e_2}(x)\big).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Both sides reduce via Theorem~\ref{thm:pointwise} to $\fin(\Rev{e_1}(x)) = \fin(\Rev{e_2}(x))$, and $\fin$ is injective.
\end{proof}

Thus the transfer is not merely sound but exactly conservative: no guarded identity is lost and no new one is gained.

\begin{definition}[Pullback]
For $g:Y\to X$, let $g^{\ast}e$ replace every atom $f$ by $f\circ g$. By induction $\Rev{g^{\ast}e}(y) = \Rev{e}(gy)$ and $\Tev{g^{\ast}e}(y) = \Tev{e}(gy)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Functoriality of the guard]
If $g:Y\to X$ is continuous and $e$ is guarded, then $g^{\ast}e$ is guarded.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction: atoms become $f\circ g$, continuous as a composite, and the nonvanishing clause transports because $\Rev{g^{\ast}e_2}(y) = \Rev{e_2}(gy)\neq0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}[The logistic function]
Let $e$ be $\exp(x)$ divided by $1+\exp(x)$. The denominator satisfies $1+e^{x}\ge 1>0$ everywhere, so $e$ is guarded, and by Theorem~\ref{thm:transfer} its transreal semantics is the continuous map $x\mapsto\fin\big(e^{x}/(1+e^{x})\big)$. Every identity it satisfies against other guarded expressions, such as $\sigma(x)+\sigma(-x)=1$, transfers in both directions by Theorem~\ref{thm:faithful}.
\end{example}

\section{The guard is invertibility}

\begin{theorem}[Multiplicative units]\label{thm:units}
For $a\in\T$, $\;\exists b\in\T,\ a\cdot b = 1\;$ if and only if $a = \fin x$ for some $x\neq0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Leftarrow$) Take $b=\fin x^{-1}$. ($\Rightarrow$) Case on $a$. If $a=\fin0$ then $a\cdot b\in\{0,\nul\}$ for all $b$. If $a=\pm\infty$ then $a\cdot b$ is an infinity or $\nul$ for every $b$ (against a finite $y$ it is $\nul$ when $y=0$ and an infinity otherwise), never the finite value $1$. If $a=\nul$ then $a\cdot b=\nul$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Additive units]
For $a\in\T$, $\;\exists b,\ a+b=0\;$ if and only if $a$ is finite.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Leftarrow$) $b=\fin(-x)$. ($\Rightarrow$) Sums involving $\pm\infty$ are infinities or $\nul$, and $\nul$ absorbs; none equals $\fin 0$.
\end{proof}

The two guards of the transfer principle---``stay finite'' and ``divide only by nonzero elements''---are therefore not chosen by hand: they are the invertible parts of the two operations.

\section{Sharpness of the guard}\label{sec:sharp}

\begin{theorem}[Guarded division is jointly continuous]\label{thm:divcont}
For $x,y\in\R$ with $y\neq0$, the map $(a,b)\mapsto a/b$ on $\T\times\T$ is continuous at $(\fin x,\fin y)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\fin\times\fin$ is an open embedding, so it identifies the neighbourhood filter of $(x,y)$ with that of $(\fin x,\fin y)$. On a neighbourhood of $(x,y)$ the second coordinate is nonzero, so there transreal division agrees with $\fin$ of real division, which is continuous at $(x,y)$ since $y\neq0$. Transport back along the open embedding.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unguarded division is jointly discontinuous]\label{thm:divdisc}
For \emph{every} $x\in\R$, the map $(a,b)\mapsto a/b$ is not continuous at $(\fin x,\fin 0)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Restrict along the continuous map $y\mapsto(\fin x,\fin y)$: continuity would make $y\mapsto \fin x/\fin y$ continuous at $0$. If $x=0$ the value at $0$ is $\nul$, and since $\{\nul\}$ is open the value would be $\nul$ on a whole neighbourhood, whereas it is $\fin 0$ for $y\neq0$. If $x\neq0$, the identity $x/y = (y/x)^{-1}$ exhibits the restriction as the patched reciprocal precomposed with the homeomorphism $y\mapsto y/x$, and Theorem~\ref{thm:norepair} applies.
\end{proof}

Together, Theorems~\ref{thm:divcont} and \ref{thm:divdisc} compute the continuity locus of division on the finite square exactly: it consists of the pairs whose second coordinate is invertible. Topology recovers the algebraic characterisation of Theorem~\ref{thm:units}.

\subsection{Rigidity}

For $v\in\T$ define the patched self-quotient $s_v(x) = v$ if $x=0$ and $s_v(x)=1$ otherwise.

\begin{lemma}[Punctured constants]\label{lem:punct}
Let $t$ be any $T_1$ topology on $\T$ and $h:\R\to(\T,t)$ continuous with $h(x)=v$ for all $x\neq0$. Then $h(0)=v$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\{v\}$ is closed, so $h^{-1}(\{v\})$ is closed and contains the dense set $\R\setminus\{0\}$, hence is all of $\R$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The unique repair value of $0/0$ is $1$]\label{thm:rigid}
For every $T_1$ topology on $\T$ and every $v\in\T$: $s_v$ is continuous if and only if $v=1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Leftarrow$) $s_1$ is the constant $1$. ($\Rightarrow$) Lemma~\ref{lem:punct}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Arithmetic refuses the repair]\label{cor:refuse}
Total transreal arithmetic assigns $0/0=\nul\neq1$ (Corollary~\ref{cor:selfdiv}). Hence $x\mapsto \fin x/\fin x$ is discontinuous for \emph{every} $T_1$ topology on the carrier: the unguarded principle fails by exactly one point and exactly one value, and no re-topologisation can repair it.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Sharpness of the transfer principle]\label{cor:sharp}
The expression $\mathrm{atom}(\mathrm{id})/\mathrm{atom}(\mathrm{id})$ is weakly guarded but its transreal semantics is discontinuous, for every $T_1$ topology. Hence the nowhere-vanishing clause cannot be deleted from Definition~\ref{def:guard} without destroying Theorem~\ref{thm:transfer}.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{No value repairs the reciprocal}

Define $r_v(y) = v$ if $y=0$ and $r_v(y)=\fin y^{-1}$ otherwise.

\begin{lemma}[Two-sided blow-up]\label{lem:blowup}
Suppose $r_v$ is continuous at $0$ in the natural topology and let $V\ni v$ be open. Then for every $M>0$ there exist $a>M$ and $b<-M$ with $\fin a\in V$ and $\fin b\in V$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Continuity gives an $\varepsilon$-ball around $0$ mapped into $V$. Choose $0<y<\min(\varepsilon,(|M|+1)^{-1})$; then $y^{-1}>M$ and $\fin y^{-1}\in V$, while $-y$ lies in the same ball and $(-y)^{-1}<-M$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Non-repairability of the reciprocal]\label{thm:norepair}
For every $v\in\T$, $r_v$ is discontinuous at $0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:blowup} with a suitable neighbourhood of $v$. If $v=\fin r$, take the image of $(r-1,r+1)$: its finite elements are bounded above by $r+1$, contradicting arbitrarily large positive $a$. If $v=+\infty$, take the image of $(0,+\infty]$: its finite elements are positive, contradicting $b<0$. If $v=-\infty$, symmetric. If $v=\nul$, take $V=\{\nul\}$, which is open and contains no finite element.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The transreal reciprocal $y\mapsto 1/\fin y$ is the case $v=+\infty$, hence discontinuous. Adjoining two infinities and a nullity to $\R$ is not enough to make unguarded division continuous.
\end{corollary}

\section{The pole trichotomy}\label{sec:poles}

Throughout, $f,g:\R\to\R$, $x_0\in\R$, and $q(x) := \fin(f x)/\fin(g x)$.

\begin{lemma}[Signed blow-up limits]\label{lem:limits}
Let $\ell$ be a filter on $\R$. If $f\to C>0$ along $\ell$ and $g\to0$ with $g>0$ eventually along $\ell$, then $q\to+\infty$ in $\T$. Symmetrically, with $g<0$ eventually, $q\to-\infty$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Eventually $g>0$, so eventually $q = \fin(f g^{-1})$; and $g^{-1}\to+\infty$, so $fg^{-1}\to+\infty$ because $f$ has positive limit; finally $\fin t\to+\infty$ in $\T$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:top}(5).
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Regime 1: the nullity jump]\label{thm:regime1}
Suppose $f(x_0)=g(x_0)=0$ and $g\neq0$ on a punctured neighbourhood of $x_0$. Then $q$ is discontinuous at $x_0$. No regularity hypothesis on $f$ or $g$ is required.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$q(x_0)=0/0=\nul$. If $q$ were continuous at $x_0$ then, $\{\nul\}$ being open (Corollary~\ref{cor:fibre}), $q$ would be $\nul$ on a whole neighbourhood; but at punctured points $g\neq0$, so $q=\fin(f/g)$ is finite there.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Regime 2: one-signed poles transfer]\label{thm:regime2}
Suppose $f$ and $g$ are continuous at $x_0$, $f(x_0)>0$, $g(x_0)=0$, and $g>0$ on a punctured neighbourhood of $x_0$. Then $q$ \emph{is} continuous at $x_0$, with $q(x_0)=+\infty$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The value is $\fin(f x_0)/0 = +\infty$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:boundary}. The limit along the punctured neighbourhood filter is $+\infty$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:limits}, and the two agree.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$x\mapsto \fin 1/\fin(x^{2})$ is a \emph{continuous} map $\R\to\T$, with value $+\infty$ at the origin.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Off the origin it agrees locally with $\fin(1/x^{2})$; at the origin apply Theorem~\ref{thm:regime2} with $f\equiv1$ and $g(x)=x^{2}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[The convention $1/0=+\infty$ breaks the sign symmetry]
The positivity of $g$ in Theorem~\ref{thm:regime2} is not cosmetic. The reciprocal is total by the
convention $\recip(0)=+\infty$, so the value assigned at $x_0$ is $\fin(f x_0)/0$, which depends on
the sign of the \emph{numerator} only: the arithmetic cannot see from which side the denominator
vanished. If $g<0$ on a punctured neighbourhood --- say $q(x)=1/(-x^{2})$ --- then both one-sided
limits are $-\infty$ while the assigned value is $+\infty$, so the quotient is discontinuous even
though the pole is perfectly one-signed. Continuity in regime~2 therefore requires the denominator
to approach zero from above.

There is a sharp corollary about presentation. The expressions $1/(-x^{2})$ and $(-1)/x^{2}$ define
the same real function off the origin, yet their total evaluations at the origin are $+\infty$ and
$-\infty$ respectively, and only the second is continuous. Outside the guard the total semantics is
a function of the \emph{syntax}, not of the real function computed; the guard is exactly the
condition under which that dependence disappears (Theorem~\ref{thm:pointwise}), which is why
faithfulness (Theorem~\ref{thm:faithful}) is a guarded statement.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Regime 3: sign change destroys continuity]\label{thm:regime3}
Suppose $f,g$ are continuous at $x_0$, $f(x_0)>0$, $g(x_0)=0$, $g>0$ on a right-punctured neighbourhood and $g<0$ on a left-punctured one. Then $q$ is discontinuous at $x_0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:limits} the one-sided limits are $+\infty$ and $-\infty$. Continuity would force both to equal $q(x_0)$, impossible in a Hausdorff space since $+\infty\neq-\infty$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$x\mapsto \fin1/\fin x$ is discontinuous at $0$: the archetypal singularity is regime 3.
\end{corollary}

