$f(x_0)\neq0$, $g<0$ near $x_0$ & $\pm\infty$ (sign of $f$) & No (limits have the opposite sign) \\
$f(x_0)\neq0$, $g$ changes sign at $x_0$ & $\pm\infty$ & No \\
$g(x_0)\neq0$ & finite & Yes (guarded case) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{The exact boundary of unguarded transfer.}
\end{table}

The nowhere-vanishing guard is therefore \emph{sufficient} (Theorem~\ref{thm:transfer}) and cannot be deleted (Corollary~\ref{cor:sharp}), but it is not \emph{necessary}; the necessary and sufficient local condition at an isolated denominator zero is the second row.

\section{The price of totality}

\begin{theorem}[$\infty-\infty$]
Total addition $\T\times\T\to\T$ is not continuous at $(+\infty,-\infty)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Along $t\mapsto(\fin t,\fin(-t))$ as $t\to+\infty$ the arguments converge to $(+\infty,-\infty)$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:top}(5), while the sums are constantly $\fin0$. Continuity would give $\fin0=\nul$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$0\cdot\infty$]
Total multiplication is not continuous at $(\fin 0,+\infty)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Along $t\mapsto(\fin t^{-1},\fin t)$ as $t\to+\infty$ the arguments converge to $(\fin0,+\infty)$ while the products are eventually constantly $1$. Continuity would give $1=\nul$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Distributivity fails]
It is not the case that $a(b+c)=ab+ac$ for all $a,b,c\in\T$: with $a=+\infty$, $b=1$, $c=0$ one has $a(b+c)=+\infty$ but $ab+ac=(+\infty)+\nul=\nul$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
All three failures are confined to the exceptional constructors. On the finite fragment---where the transfer principle lives---the field axioms hold verbatim and both operations are jointly continuous. The exceptional elements are exactly the price of totality, and the guard is exactly the condition that avoids paying it.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Guard checking.} Given a syntax tree and, at each division node, a certificate that the denominator is nowhere zero, guardedness is decided by one post-order traversal conjoining the continuity obligations of atoms and compositions with the nonvanishing obligations of denominators. The cost is $O(n)$ in the number of nodes, plus the cost of the certificates, which in practice are simple positivity facts such as $1+e^{x}\ge1$ or $1+x^{2}\ge1$.

\paragraph{Transreal evaluation with regime classification.} Evaluate bottom-up in the four-constructor arithmetic, propagating one of four tags (finite, $+\infty$, $-\infty$, $\nul$). At a division node whose denominator vanishes, classify the local regime by sampling the denominator's sign on both sides: numerator also zero $\Rightarrow$ regime 1 ($\nul$, discontinuous); denominator of constant positive sign $\Rightarrow$ regime 2 ($\pm\infty$, continuous); denominator of constant negative sign $\Rightarrow$ regime $2'$ ($\pm\infty$ with the wrong sign, discontinuous); denominator sign-changing $\Rightarrow$ regime 3 ($\pm\infty$, discontinuous). This gives an $O(n)$ evaluation plus a continuity verdict which, by Theorems~\ref{thm:regime1}--\ref{thm:regime3}, is exact whenever the sampled signs are the true one-sided signs.

\section{Applications}

\subsection{Exception-free arithmetic in cryptographic implementations}

In elliptic-curve cryptography a point is stored projectively as $(X:Y:Z)$ and the affine coordinates are recovered as $x=X/Z$, $y=Y/Z$. The group identity---the point at infinity---is exactly the locus $Z=0$, where those divisions are unguarded. An implementation that divides must branch on $Z=0$, and a branch whose condition depends on secret data is a timing side channel; the ``complete addition formula'' literature exists to avoid precisely this.

Total arithmetic offers an alternative: never branch, and let the exceptional constructor record the exceptional case. Our results delimit exactly what that buys.
\begin{itemize}
 \item \emph{Inside the guard} (a ladder arranged so that $Z\neq0$ throughout), Theorems~\ref{thm:pointwise}--\ref{thm:faithful} say the totalisation is invisible: identical values, identical equational theory, continuity preserved.
 \item \emph{At the boundary}, Corollary~\ref{cor:refuse} and Theorem~\ref{thm:norepair} say the exceptional value is a genuine discontinuity that no re-topologisation and no repair value can smooth away. A totalised implementation is correct but not stable at $Z=0$: in fixed precision a value near the boundary can flip between an infinity and a nullity on rounding noise, so no continuity-based argument (smoothness, Lipschitz bounds, interpolation) may be built on it.
 \item \emph{Regime 2} (Theorem~\ref{thm:regime2}) identifies the benign unguarded situations: an even-order denominator zero at which the denominator stays positive, with nonvanishing numerator, degrades gracefully to a signed infinity, whereas an odd-order zero, a negative even-order zero, or a coincident zero does not.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Numerical software and machine learning}

Activation functions, normalisation layers and softmax denominators are guarded by construction ($1+e^{x}$, $\sum_i e^{z_i}$, $\varepsilon+\sigma^{2}$), and Theorem~\ref{thm:transfer} certifies that implementing them in a total arithmetic cannot change values or break continuity. Conversely, Theorem~\ref{thm:regime1} names the dangerous pattern: a ratio of two quantities that vanish together---an unregularised $0/0$ in a masked attention weight, an empty-bucket average---is exactly the regime whose failure needs no regularity hypotheses at all.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

\subsection{What has been settled}
Guarded transfer holds (Theorem~\ref{thm:transfer}), is exact in both directions (Theorem~\ref{thm:faithful}), is functorial, and its guard is sharp for every $T_1$ topology (Corollary~\ref{cor:sharp}); the guard itself is the unit group (Theorem~\ref{thm:units}) and the exact continuity locus (Theorems~\ref{thm:divcont}--\ref{thm:divdisc}). The hedge ``generally'' in the failure clause is justified and quantified by the pole trichotomy.

\subsection{Open directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Uniqueness of the natural topology]
Let $t$ be a compact Hausdorff topology on the four-constructor carrier for which $\fin:\R\to\T$ is an open embedding and $\{\nul\}$ is open. Then $t$ is the natural topology, i.e.\ the identity is a homeomorphism onto $\Rbar\sqcup\{\ast\}$.
\end{conjecture}

The key insight is that a compact Hausdorff space containing $\R$ as an open subset with a two-point remainder must be the end compactification, because the line has exactly two ends and each remainder point must absorb one of them. The present development \emph{chose} a topology and proved the guard sharp for it; uniqueness would upgrade ``a natural topology'' to ``the natural topology''.

\begin{conjecture}[Complete characterisation of guardedness up to semantics]
For every expression all of whose atoms and compositions are continuous, the transreal evaluation is continuous if and only if at every zero $x_0$ of every denominator subexpression the local data fall in regime 2 of the pole trichotomy: numerator nonzero at $x_0$, denominator positive on a punctured neighbourhood.
\end{conjecture}

The key insight is that the pole trichotomy is local and the syntax is finite, so continuity should be decidable by a finite conjunction of local regime tests; the technical obstacle is nesting, since a denominator may itself take exceptional values.

\paragraph{Higher dimensions and complex poles.} The two-point compactification is special to the line: $\R^{n}$ for $n\ge2$ has one end and $\mathbb{C}$ compactifies to a sphere with a single $\infty$. Over $\mathbb{C}$ the analogue of the trichotomy should read: $0/0$ breaks, and every isolated pole transfers, because the Riemann sphere has no sign to change. If so, the sign-changing regime is a purely one-dimensional, purely ordered phenomenon.

\paragraph{Quantitative stability.} Continuity is qualitative. Near a regime-2 pole one would like an explicit modulus of continuity in a metric inducing the natural topology (for instance the arctangent metric on $\Rbar$ with $\nul$ at distance $1$ from everything), turning the qualitative guard into a numerical error bound.

\paragraph{Beyond arithmetic.} Only $+,\cdot,-,/$ are extended by hand; other real functions are lifted strictly. Lifting instead by limits at $\pm\infty$ where they exist ($\exp(-\infty)=0$, $\arctan(+\infty)=\pi/2$) would enlarge the guarded fragment; characterising which functions admit such a continuous lift, and whether transfer stays exactly conservative, is open.

\section{Conclusion}

Total arithmetic is not a swindle, but neither is it free. On the guarded fragment---finite values, and division only by nowhere-vanishing denominators---totalisation is completely invisible: the same values, the same equational theory in both directions, and continuity into a compact Hausdorff carrier.

The guard that makes this work is not an artefact. It is the unit group of the multiplication, and the exact continuity locus of the division map, and it cannot be weakened: unguarded self-division is discontinuous for every $T_1$ topology on the carrier, and unguarded reciprocation cannot be repaired by any value in the carrier. Past the guard lies not chaos but a trichotomy: coincident zeros always break, sign-changing poles always break, and positive one-signed poles do not break at all, so that $1/x^{2}$ extends to a continuous map of the whole line into the four-constructor space.

Dividing by zero is permitted. It merely costs continuity, at three named points---$0/0$, $\infty-\infty$, $0\cdot\infty$---and nowhere else.

\end{document}

